{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nEvery change to the plan-review gate — a Pass-1 criterion prompt, a Pass-2 impact/validity question, a Pass-3 formula or threshold — lands on a handful of live dogfood reviews. No standing instrument answers \"did this change make the gate better or worse?\" before it ships; the prior calibration epic (`pastoral-aquatic-viper`, 6982) rejected live A/B batches on cost and parked the human gold set. The work is scattered across six epics and several orphan stories (`jira-reb-529`, `jira-reb-1589`, `fort-wisp-wren`, `spin-cub-usher`, `ridiculous-expert-mouse`, `auspicious-torrid-curlew`). This epic unifies them behind one harness.\n\nThe instrument already mostly exists as data. The live rebar store holds ~4,300 `plan_review_result_v2` sidecars on ~1,280 tickets (2026-06-27 → now; ~2,000 older ones pruned from the tree but recoverable from `origin/tickets` history) and `~/legacy-modernization-navigator` holds 74 more. Each v2 sidecar retains the raw Pass-1 findings (prose + evidence + scenarios + criteria), the complete Pass-2 answer vector (graded binaries + severity attributes), and the Pass-3 outputs (validity, impact, priority, the exact `block_threshold`, decision). The reviewed plan text is not stored but is reconstructable from ticket `EDIT`/`SNAPSHOT` history and matchable against the stored `material_fingerprint` — a hash over a NORMALIZED, PARTIAL basis (`material_diff.material_basis`: ticket_id, canonical description with checkbox-state and insignificant-whitespace normalized away, file_impact, sorted child ids, optional no-file-impact reason), so a match establishes normalized-material equivalence, not byte-exact reconstruction of the reviewed text. The store also carries free outcome labels: 2,483 revise→re-review pairs (finding survival via `norm_id`), 208 verdict flips on byte-identical material (the reviewer-noise floor), 84 `plan_defect` bug closes, 89 `caused_by` links, 199 plan-PASS→completion-FAIL tickets, 18 reopens.\n\n## Approach\n\nThree eval tiers, ordered by cost, each measuring one pass in isolation while holding the others fixed:\n\n- **Tier 0 — Pass-3 replay (zero LLM).** Re-run `review_kernel.decide.pass3_decide` (and the post-verdict floors) over the whole corpus with a candidate formula/threshold/routing overlay; diff against stored decisions. Generalises `docs/calibration/plan_v5_rescore.py` and `docs/experiments/calibrate_plan_review_thresholds.py`. Runs on every proposal, unlimited.\n- **Tier 1 — Pass-2 replay (Sonnet, ~$0.5/review uncached).** Re-run the verifier with candidate questions/prompt on the *stored* Pass-1 findings plus reconstructed plan text via the `verify_findings(run_chunk=…)` seam; score per-question agreement against stored answers, then feed Tier 0. The model re-run consumes the same normalized material the corpus builder reconstructs and verifies (see Context) — normalized-basis equivalence is what the replay needs, not byte-exact text.\n- **Tier 2 — Pass-1 replay (Opus, ~$3.4/review uncached, plus a Tier-1 run on the new findings).** Re-run finders with candidate criteria on a fixed stratified sample (~20 tickets); compare finding sets by `norm_id`/criterion; single-criterion mode (~$0.17/call) for per-criterion edits.\n\nNon-negotiable invariants, enforced by the harness rather than by convention:\n\n1. **Hard budget.** A committed ledger tracks every eval call priced via `genai-prices`; every run pre-flights a cost estimate and refuses to start if projected spend would exceed the remaining allocation. Epic-wide cap is **$200** total with a $30 reserve; commissioning spend is planned at ≤$120 (one Tier-1 reproduction run to measure verifier noise, one Tier-2 baseline sample). Per-proposal costs are published.\n2. **Model parity.** Every LLM re-run uses the exact production model class for that pass (frontier Opus for Pass 1, standard Sonnet for Pass 2) through **Bedrock only**, on a homogeneous provider chain (the mixed Bedrock+Anthropic fallback silently disables prompt caching and roughly doubles cost). The harness records the per-pass model id in every result, refuses to diff runs whose per-pass model ids differ, and refuses to start when the configured model differs from the corpus baseline's `provider_provenance.ran_model`. Substituting a cheaper model to save budget is forbidden: it confounds the measurement.\n3. **Offline first.** Tier 0 and all label joins are pure functions of the frozen corpus; no proposal may be assessed on a live-only signal. Consistent with ADR 0054 (the field corpus is the instrument) and with 6982's rejection of live A/B.\n4. **Noise-aware acceptance.** A change is reported as regressing/improving only when its label-proxy deltas exceed the noise band measured from identical-material flips and the Tier-1 reproduction run; otherwise \"indistinguishable\".\n\n## Scope\n\nNew package `src/rebar/llm/evals/plan_replay/` (corpus, labels, tier runners, ledger, report) with a `python -m rebar.llm.evals.plan_replay` entry; frozen corpus manifest + ledger + reports under `docs/experiments/plan-review-gate/replay/`; an ADR and a contributor guide section; fixes to `usage_log` ticket attribution. No CI provider dependency — everything runs locally.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A frozen, versioned replay corpus exists covering both stores (tree + git-recovered sidecars), with at-review plan text reconstructed and material-fingerprint-equivalent (matching under the normalized basis, across the full legacy generation ladder — see `celestine-treasonous-ewe`) for a majority of v2 sidecars; the corpus builder reports the observed ratio without asserting a hard threshold in this epic, and that observed ratio is the input the labels/noise-floor work (`expectable-clownlike-ynambu`) uses to decide whether and where a floor is warranted; verified by the corpus manifest's coverage counts and `verified_ratio`.\n- [ ] Outcome labels and a reviewer-noise floor are computed from the corpus with no LLM calls and joined to every review/finding; verified by a labels report listing counts per label source.\n- [ ] A Pass-3 change (formula, threshold, routing overlay) can be assessed over the entire corpus with zero LLM calls, producing a diff report (verdict flips, per-criterion deltas, label-proxy precision/recall, friction rate); verified by replaying current code against itself with zero diffs and replaying a known historical threshold change reproducing its documented effect.\n- [ ] A Pass-2 change can be assessed on a stratified sample using stored Pass-1 findings, with per-question agreement vs stored answers and downstream Tier-0 metrics; verified by a reproduction run (unchanged questions) whose agreement establishes the noise floor.\n- [ ] A Pass-1 change can be assessed on a fixed stratified sample with finding-set comparison by `norm_id`/criterion and downstream Tier-1/Tier-0 metrics; verified by one baseline sample run.\n- [ ] Every LLM eval call is priced and written to a committed ledger; a run whose pre-flight estimate exceeds remaining allocation is refused; total ledger spend at epic close is ≤ $200; verified by the ledger file and a test of the refusal path.\n- [ ] Every eval result records the per-pass model id; the harness refuses to diff results with differing model ids and refuses to run a pass on a model other than its production class via Bedrock; verified by unit tests and by the ledger showing only Bedrock Opus/Sonnet ids.\n- [ ] Prompt caching is confirmed active for eval runs (cache_read_tokens > 0 on the second call of a run); verified from the usage log.\n- [ ] A contributor-facing protocol (ADR + docs) states which tier a proposal needs, the required report artifact, and the noise-band acceptance rule; verified by the docs landing and being referenced from `docs/plan-review-gate.md`.\n\n## Child decomposition\n\nCorpus builder (criterion 1) → outcome labels + noise floor (2) → Tier-0 harness (3) → Tier-1 harness (4) → Tier-2 harness (5) → budget ledger + model-parity guard + homogeneous Bedrock chain (6, 7, 8; includes the usage-log ticket-attribution bug) → change protocol ADR + docs (9). Folded-in existing work: `ridiculous-expert-mouse` (per-criterion fixture eval, a Tier-2 single-criterion consumer), `auspicious-torrid-curlew` (deterministic-floor candidates mined via Tier-0 replay), `brutal-idiocratic-rodent` (optional human-adjudicated gold subset, deferred enrichment). `jira-reb-529` is superseded: its recorded-LLM-response mocking is infeasible because prompts/responses are never persisted; the sidecar-replay tiers deliver its intent. `jira-reb-1589` and `fort-wisp-wren` are consumers that depend on this harness; `spin-cub-usher` and `yip-gem-teak` are related but out of scope.\n\n## Non-goals\n\nChanging any production criterion, question, or threshold (this epic builds the instrument); a full human-adjudicated gold set; live A/B in the production gate."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788045597780604681,"uuid":"8c7cedd2-9e72-47b6-a2c2-9e950dc52c45"}